// =============================================================================
// 21-apim-206-semantic-partial-capture.kql
// The only mechanism anywhere in Azure Monitor that recovers the 206 reason.
// =============================================================================
//
// RUN THE DISCOVERY QUERIES FIRST
//   00-discover-dependency-types.kql and 01-discover-search-diagnostic-shape.kql.
//   File 01 section 3 tells you whether the Search resource log sees 206 at all;
//   this file is the fallback when it does not, and the reason-level detail even
//   when it does.
//
// PURPOSE
//   Extract @search.semanticPartialResponseReason from the captured response
//   body on every 206 crossing the APIM gateway, and report the distribution of
//   reasons over time.
//
// PREREQUISITES, AND ONE OF THEM IS HARD. READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING.
//   This query returns nothing useful unless RESPONSE-BODY LOGGING is enabled on
//   the APIM GatewayLogs diagnostic setting. BackendResponseBody and ResponseBody
//   are empty by default. Bodies are truncated to the configured byte limit, so
//   set the limit high enough that the @search.* annotations, which sit near the
//   start of a Search response, are retained.
//   Body logging carries privacy and DLP implications. Get that reviewed before
//   turning it on in production.
//   Enable with: scripts/Enable-DiagnosticSettings.ps1
//
// WHY APIM IS THE ONLY ROUTE
//   @search.semanticPartialResponseReason is NOT in the Azure AI Search resource
//   log schema. The documented Properties sub-schema contains exactly four
//   fields: Description_s, Documents_d, IndexName_s, Query_s. The reason string
//   exists only in the HTTP response body returned to the caller. APIM sits in
//   the path and can capture that body; nothing else in the telemetry chain can.
//   This is the single strongest argument for keeping APIM in the path.
//   Research: .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md
//             Lines 188-200 (D11 schema limitation), Lines 338-349 (Q8d)
//
// WHAT THIS QUERY DECIDES (open items U1 and U2)
//   U1: the sampled 206 carried reason "Transient". Microsoft documents
//       "CapacityOverloaded" as the capacity signal and says nothing linking
//       "Transient" to tier or capacity. If this query shows CapacityOverloaded
//       in the population, the capacity story is PROVEN. If every row is
//       Transient, the capacity story remains INFERRED and belongs in the
//       support ticket as question one.
//   U2: the semanticErrorHandling parameter defaults to "fail", which returns an
//       error rather than a 206. Something in the path is setting it to
//       "partial", and the built-in connector does not expose it. Inspecting the
//       captured REQUEST body identifies who sets it. See COMPANION B.
//
// TARGET TABLE
//   ApiManagementGatewayLogs.
//
// UNVERIFIED ASSUMPTION
//   The extract() pattern below assumes the body literally contains
//   "semanticPartialResponseReason":"<value>". Microsoft documents the phrases
//   but does not publish the exact JSON body shape. Capture one real body with
//   COMPANION A and adjust the pattern if the serialization differs.
//
// HEALTHY VS UNHEALTHY
//   Healthy   : zero rows.
//   Unhealthy : any rows at all. Every 206 is a query that returned base results
//               without semantic re-ranking, which the user experiences as a
//               worse or empty answer rather than as an error.
//   Escalate  : Reasons contains "CapacityOverloaded". Microsoft's own guidance
//               for workloads at that ceiling is to file a support ticket so the
//               workload can be provisioned for.
//
// Derived from Q8d-Apim206SemanticPartialCapture,
//   .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md Lines 339-349
// =============================================================================

let lookback = 24h;
let bucket = 1h;
ApiManagementGatewayLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where ResponseCode == 206 or BackendResponseCode == 206
| extend
    PartialReason = extract(@"semanticPartialResponseReason""\s*:\s*""([^""]+)""", 1, tostring(BackendResponseBody)),
    PartialType   = extract(@"semanticPartialResponseType""\s*:\s*""([^""]+)""", 1, tostring(BackendResponseBody))
| summarize
    Count        = count(),
    Reasons      = make_set(PartialReason, 10),
    Types        = make_set(PartialType, 10),
    Apis         = make_set(ApiId, 10),
    AvgBackendMs = round(avg(BackendTime), 1),
    P95BackendMs = round(percentile(BackendTime, 95), 1),
    FirstSeen    = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen     = max(TimeGenerated)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, bucket)
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION A: capture one raw body and validate the regex
// Run this first, once. Read the BodySnippet column and confirm the annotation
// is serialized the way the main query expects before trusting any aggregate.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 24h;
// ApiManagementGatewayLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where ResponseCode == 206 or BackendResponseCode == 206
// | project TimeGenerated, ApiId, OperationName, ResponseCode, BackendResponseCode, BackendTime,
//           BodySnippet = substring(tostring(BackendResponseBody), 0, 2000)
// | order by TimeGenerated desc
// | take 5

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION B: who is setting semanticErrorHandling to partial? (open item U2)
// The documented default is "fail". A 206 therefore proves something upstream
// set it to "partial". This inspects the OUTBOUND request body APIM sent to
// search.windows.net and also surfaces the effective semanticMaxWaitInMilliseconds,
// whose value when omitted is undocumented (open item U3).
// Requires request-body logging in addition to response-body logging.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 24h;
// ApiManagementGatewayLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where BackendUrl has "search.windows.net"
// | extend
//     ErrorHandling = extract(@"semanticErrorHandling""\s*:\s*""([^""]+)""", 1, tostring(BackendRequestBody)),
//     MaxWaitMs     = extract(@"semanticMaxWaitInMilliseconds""\s*:\s*(\d+)", 1, tostring(BackendRequestBody)),
//     QueryType     = extract(@"queryType""\s*:\s*""([^""]+)""", 1, tostring(BackendRequestBody))
// | summarize Count = count(), SampleRequest = take_any(substring(tostring(BackendRequestBody), 0, 1500))
//     by ErrorHandling, MaxWaitMs, QueryType, ApiId, OperationName
// | order by Count desc

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION C: 206 rate over time against total Search-bound traffic
// Gives the percentage figure to alert on. Any sustained non-zero value is
// worth investigating; the specific threshold is an engineering judgement
// because Microsoft publishes none.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 7d;
// ApiManagementGatewayLogs
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where BackendUrl has "search.windows.net"
// | summarize
//     Total   = count(),
//     Partial = countif(ResponseCode == 206 or BackendResponseCode == 206)
//     by bin(TimeGenerated, 15m)
// | extend PartialPct = round(100.0 * Partial / Total, 2)
// | project TimeGenerated, Total, Partial, PartialPct
// | render timechart with (ytitle = "206 partial responses as percent of Search traffic")
